. Writing help text... Writing the link creation logic...\n\nI'm noticing these helper functions are pretty thin wrappers around the same core operation, so I should consolidate them to keep the code cleaner and stay within the line limits. Let me think about whether the `run` function can handle this more compactly without sacrificing readability. Writing the createLink function...\n\nI need to handle error formatting to match GNU ln's output exactly. When a hard link or symlink fails because the destination already exists, I should return errors formatted as \"ln: failed to create hard link 'LINK_NAME': File exists\" or \"ln: failed to create symbolic link 'LINK_NAME': File exists\" so the stderr output matches what the tests expect. I need to match GNU ln's error message format exactly for the tests to pass. Looking at the coreutils source, the pattern is `ln: failed to create hard link 'DEST': ERROR_MESSAGE`, where the error message comes from the system's strerror function. I'm examining what the exact output should be for different error cases like file exists and directory operations. Looking at the error message format for when a target doesn't exist, GNU ln outputs `ln: failed to access 'TARGET': No such file or directory`. For the destination directory check, I'm using `os.Stat` which follows symlinks, so symlinks to directories are correctly treated as directories—this matches GNU ln's default behavior. I also need to handle the case where symbolic links don't require the target to exist, unlike hard links. Writing the link creation functions... I need to handle the error unwrapping properly—Go's `os.Link` returns a `*LinkError` that wraps the underlying syscall error, so I should use `errors.As` or access the `Err` field directly to extract just the OS error message. The tricky part is matching the capitalization that GNU ln uses; on macOS, the syscall returns \"file exists\" in lowercase, but GNU coreutils formats it with a capital letter like \"File exists\".\n\nSince differential testing is a later task, I'll keep this simple for now and just extract the underlying error message from Go's error types without worrying about exact capitalization matching.\n\nExtracting the error message... I'm realizing that Go's default error messages won't match GNU ln's format, so I need to wrap and format them properly. I'm writing helper functions to convert system errors into the expected format, then applying them consistently across the link creation logic. I'm noticing there's a discrepancy between how Go's syscall errors are formatted on macOS versus what the standard C `strerror()` function returns—Go gives lowercase strings like \"file exists\" while the system call returns capitalized versions. For now I'll stick with what Go provides and let the differential tests catch any case mismatches later. I should probably look at how other command-line utilities handle this to see if there's a standard approach.\n\nWriting the ln implementation... Implementing the argument parser logic... Writing flag parsing functions...\n\nNow I'm checking the `isDirectory` function, which uses `os.Stat` to follow symlinks as expected for the default behavior. I'm also thinking through edge cases like what happens when `ln` is called with no arguments or just a target, and whether GNU `ln` actually supports creating a link in the current directory with the target's basename.\n\nLooking at the specification and GNU `ln` behavior, it seems the single-target form isn't supported—it requires at least two arguments. But I notice the help text I was planning includes a usage line for that form, which contradicts what the actual behavior should be.\n\nLet me verify this against GNU `ln`'s actual help output and source code to understand what the single-target form actually does when provided. I'm testing whether the single-target form works with symbolic links—`ln -s /usr/bin/sort` should create `./sort` as a symlink. The SRD doesn't explicitly require this form, but it's consistent with GNU ln behavior and doesn't conflict with the non-goals around `-t` and `-T` flags, so I'll support it.\n\nNow I'm checking the error messages GNU ln produces: with no arguments it says \"missing file operand\" followed by a help hint, and when given multiple targets where the last isn't a directory, it says \"target 'X' is not a directory\". I should match these messages in my implementation.\n\nI'm torn on whether to include all the flags in the help text and parser even though I'm only implementing -s for now. Including them would match gln's output exactly, but then the parser would accept flags that don't actually do anything, which feels misleading. On the other hand, only including -s keeps the behavior consistent with what's actually implemented, even if the help text doesn't fully match gln. Since this is a single task generating one command at a time, I think it makes more sense to stick with just -s for now and let future tasks add the other flags.\n\nBut wait—looking at how the basename example handles this, it includes the complete help text from the start, including flags that might not all be implemented yet. So maybe the right approach is to include all the SRD flags in the help text and parser, store them in the options struct, but only implement behavior for the in-scope ones. That way the help text is complete and future tasks can fill in the functionality without changing the help text again.\n\nActually, that's adding features beyond the task scope. I should stick to what's required: just the `-s` flag and basic usage information. The help text will be minimal for now and can expand as new tasks add more functionality.\n\nI'm settling on a minimal help text that covers the `-s` flag and the three usage forms, with the standard `--help` and `--version` options. - R2.1-R2.3 (symbolic links with -s, not -r)\n- Infrastructure (SIGPIPE, --version, --help)\n\nAnd excludes:\n- R3 (force/interactive/backup/verbose/no-dereference)\n- R2.4 (relative symlinks)\n- R4 (differential tests)\n\nSo I need to implement -s for basic symbolic link creation and the infrastructure pieces, but skip the flag parsers for -f, -i, -n, -v, -b, -S, -r since they're out of scope. The test command should work, though the differential tests themselves aren't part of this task. I'm second-guessing whether R2 is actually in scope, but looking at the task summary mentioning \"basic hard and symbolic links\" with the -s flag, and the requirements section explicitly calling out R2.1 for symbolic link creation, it's clear R2.1-R2.3 are included. I've been overthinking this—let me just write the implementation.\n\nWriting the ln command implementation... Writing the link creation logic... Writing link creation functions... Continuing with the argument parsing logic...\n\nNow I'm handling the long flag parsing by checking for unrecognized options and returning an error, then moving into the short flags handler which iterates through each character and validates them against supported options like the symbolic flag. I should verify that I have all the necessary imports including the errors package for error handling, along with fmt, and os for file operations and output.\n\nLet me check the function sizes to make sure they stay under the 40-line limit—main is around 10 lines, run is about 25, and the helper functions like createLink, createHardLink, and the parsing functions are all well under that threshold, with the total file coming in around 140-150 lines.\n\nI'm noticing an issue with the `parseLongFlag` function though—after calling `os.Exit(0)` in the help and version cases, the subsequent `return` statements are unreachable, which could trigger compiler warnings. I need to restructure this to handle the exit calls properly without leaving dead code. Adding return statements... Writing the flag parser...\n\nNow I'm verifying the edge cases for the ln implementation—checking that it handles missing