 I need to handle both error types separately in the `sysError` function. Both `*os.PathError` and `*os.LinkError` implement `Unwrap()`, so I could simplify the function to just unwrap errors until reaching the base case and return its string representation—though the explicit type checking approach is probably clearer about what I'm doing.\n\nActually, the unwrap loop is more idiomatic Go and handles any wrapped error type, not just those two. I'll go with that approach.\n\nNow I'm considering whether `errors.As` with `syscall.Errno` would be more direct, but that requires an extra import. The unwrap loop is cleaner without adding dependencies.\n\nLet me verify the edge cases: `parseArgs` will return an error if the file operand is missing, so I don't need to handle empty arguments separately. I think I'm ready to write the file now.\n\nActually, let me double-check the task requirements one more time to make sure I'm covering everything—hard links, multi-target directory form, errors for hard links to directories, destination existence checks, symbolic links with `-s`, directory detection, and the signal handler plus version and help flags. I don't need a test file since the task only requires main.go, and running `go test` on a package without tests will just report that there are no test files, which is acceptable. Now I'm ready to start writing the code—first I'll check if the cmd/ln directory exists, then I'll craft the help text based on GNU ln's format to cover the different usage patterns: creating a link with a specific name, creating a link in the current directory, creating multiple links in a target directory, and using the -t flag for batch operations. Looking at the rest of the help text and the SRD constraints, I need to filter out the unsupported flags like -L, -P, -t, -T, and -d/-F. The flags I should actually include are -s, -f, -i, -n, -r, -v, -b, --backup, and -S, since those align with what the SRD specifies.\n\nBut wait—I'm only implementing -s right now, so including unimplemented flags in the help text could confuse users or tests when they try to use them and get errors. It makes more sense to only document what's actually working, and let future tasks expand the help text as they add more functionality. So I'll keep it to just -s for now and build out the complete help message with the proper usage forms and descriptions.\n\nNow I need to verify that `filepath.Base` handles edge cases correctly—like when the target is just a filename with no directory path, it returns the filename as-is, which is the expected behavior. And for special cases like the root directory, the function would correctly identify it as a directory and reject hard link attempts.\n\nI'm also thinking through what happens with empty strings and other edge cases, then I need to check what GNU `ln` actually outputs when multiple targets are provided but the last one isn't a directory, so I can match that error message exactly. I've verified that our implementation handles the case where the destination directory exists but the source files don't—it correctly reports an error for each missing target, matching GNU ln's behavior. Now I'm ready to implement the code.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13292,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13292},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"acf3b1ba-a30a-4192-8bb7-2343f6098d37","uuid":"5381b204-1343-49f1-830b-2af173eddafa"}
